(window.SITE_PARTS=window.SITE_PARTS||{})["bill-firearmcommerce"]={"plain":{"bills":{"firearmcommerce":{"slug":"firearmcommerce","title":"The Gun Trafficking Act","sections":[{"anchor":"sec-0-1","heading":"\u00a7 0-1. Short title.","part":null,"body":"This Act may be cited as the \"Gun Trafficking Act.\""},{"anchor":"sec-0-2","heading":"\u00a7 0-2. Findings.","part":null,"body":"Congress finds as follows:\n\n1. Handgun purchaser licensing is the single best-evidenced firearm policy in the United States. Connecticut's 1995 permit-to-purchase law was followed by an estimated 40 percent reduction in firearm homicide and a 15 percent reduction in firearm suicide; Missouri's 2007 repeal of its licensing law was followed by increases of roughly 25 percent in firearm homicide and, in later analysis, 47 percent, with a measured rise in guns diverted to crime shortly after retail sale. The Supreme Court in *New York State Rifle & Pistol Association v. Bruen* expressly approved licensing that issues on objective criteria without open-ended discretion, exorbitant fees, or abusive delay; this Act builds exactly that regime and no other.\n2. A firearm made from a kit is a firearm. The frame-or-receiver definitions this Act codifies were upheld as a lawful reading of the Gun Control Act in *Bondi v. VanDerStok*, 604 U.S. 458 (2025); putting them in the statute itself removes the vagueness and authority questions that later litigation has raised.\n3. A device that makes a semiautomatic firearm fire like a machine gun makes it a machine gun. The National Firearms Act has governed machine guns since 1934, and lawfully registered machine guns are essentially never used in crime; the surge in recovered conversion devices is the evasion of that success, and the answer is to define the regulated class by what a device does rather than by its name.\n4. A small fraction of licensed dealers account for a large majority of guns later recovered in crime, and enforcement against them has swung with policy rather than resting on law. Every consequence in this Act for a dealer turns on a willful violation, established by the objective record.\n5. A waiting period burdens the timing of an acquisition and nothing else. A national study of every State policy change since 1970 estimates that a waiting period reduces firearm homicides by about 17 percent; its estimated reduction in firearm suicides, 7 to 11 percent, does not hold in models that include State-specific trends. The mechanism is the one every means-safety measure runs on: a crisis that passes is a death that does not occur. Because the risk attaches to each acquisition, the period applies to every acquisition, and to a permit holder as to anyone else.\n6. Nothing in this Act reaches the possession of any firearm lawfully possessed on the date of its enactment, with the single exception stated in section 3-2(b) for a part or device that becomes a machinegun by the amendment made by that section, which its owner may transfer, disable, or destroy within the period that subsection states; nothing in this Act requires the surrender, sale, or registration of any such firearm; and nothing in this Act authorizes any registry of firearms or firearm owners. Each provision of this Act is independently severable, and no provision is to be read as part of an integrated scheme with any other."},{"anchor":"sec-0-3","heading":"\u00a7 0-3. Definitions.","part":null,"body":"In this Act:\n\n**(a)** \"Firearm,\" \"handgun,\" \"licensed dealer,\" \"licensed manufacturer,\" \"licensed importer,\" and \"machinegun\" have the meanings given those terms in section 921(a) of title 18, United States Code, as amended by this Act; and \"licensee\" means a person licensed under chapter 44 of that title.\n\n**(b)** \"Transfer\" means a sale, delivery, gift, loan, or other disposition by which a person parts with possession of a firearm to another person, and \"acquire\" is its correlative; the terms do not include a temporary transfer described in section 1-2(c).\n\n**(c)** \"State\" includes the District of Columbia and each territory; Tribal governments join every enumeration of this Act that funds, protects, empowers, or recognizes, and this Act binds a Tribal government only as to a function voluntarily assumed."},{"anchor":"sec-1-1","heading":"\u00a7 1-1. Permit required to acquire a handgun.","part":"TITLE I \u2014 ACQUISITION LICENSING","body":"**(a) Rule.** Beginning eighteen months after enactment, it shall be unlawful for a person to acquire a handgun unless, at the time of acquisition, the person holds a valid acquisition permit issued under this title or under a State program certified under section 1-4.\n\n**(b) Transferor's check.** It shall be unlawful for a licensed dealer, or for any person, to transfer a handgun to a person who does not hold a valid permit. Verification of a facially valid permit against the issuing authority's confirmation system is a complete defense, and a private transferor who so verifies bears no duty to inquire further.\n\n**(c) This title reaches acquisition, not possession.** No provision of this title requires a permit to possess, carry, or continue to own a handgun lawfully possessed before the date subsection (a) takes effect, or acquired by a permit holder.","partHeads":[{"heading":"TITLE I \u2014 ACQUISITION LICENSING","body":""}]},{"anchor":"sec-1-2","heading":"\u00a7 1-2. The permit.","part":"TITLE I \u2014 ACQUISITION LICENSING","body":"**(a) Shall-issue on objective criteria.** The issuing authority shall issue an acquisition permit to an applicant who \u2014\n1. is twenty-one years of age or older, or eighteen or older and within a category (military, law enforcement) the SCHEDULE names;\n2. is not prohibited from receiving a firearm under section 922(g) or (n) of title 18, United States Code, or State law, determined by a check of the national instant criminal background check system;\n3. has completed a firearm-safety course meeting the standard of section 1-3; and\n4. has submitted fingerprints for the verification of identity this subsection describes.\nThe authority shall issue or deny within the number of days set in the SCHEDULE, counted from the day the authority receives the application, whether or not the authority then regards it as complete; the authority shall state that day to the applicant in writing, and the period runs from it whether or not the authority does so. Not later than the day after it receives the applicant's fingerprints, the authority shall submit them for the comparison subparagraph (C) describes. **If the authority has neither issued nor denied within that period, the permit is issued by operation of this subsection** where \u2014\n(A) the application states the applicant's date of birth, showing on its face that the applicant meets the age paragraph (1) requires, and, for an applicant under twenty-one, the category the SCHEDULE names within which the applicant states that the applicant falls;\n(B) the application includes a certificate of completion of a course meeting the standard of section 1-3, or the applicant's statement that no qualifying course has been reasonably available to the applicant; and\n(C) the background check has been completed, including verification of the applicant's identity against the fingerprints the applicant submitted under paragraph (4), and has returned no disqualifying record.\nThe verification of identity is completed when the comparison of the submitted fingerprints with the records the background check searches has returned its result, and no further review or inquiry is a part of it. No issuance occurs by default while the background check is pending or has returned a disqualifying record, or, except as the next sentence provides, while that verification of identity is pending. If subparagraph (A) or (B), or the requirement of subparagraph (C) that the verification of identity against the applicant's fingerprints be completed, is held invalid, in whole or as applied to an applicant or a class of applicants, then as to each applicant to whom the holding applies, the permit is issued by operation of this subsection where the authority has neither issued nor denied within that period and the background check has returned no disqualifying record, whether or not that verification of identity has been completed. A permit issued by operation of this subsection on a statement under subparagraph (A) or (B) that a court finds the applicant knew to be false is void from issuance, and no issuing authority may treat it as void before that finding. The duties to submit the fingerprints for comparison, to furnish the applicant evidence of a permit issued by operation of this subsection, and to show that permit as valid in the confirmation system section 1-1(b) names, are ministerial, are owed to the applicant, and are enforceable by mandamus.\n\n**(b) Objective denial only.** The authority may deny only for a failure of a criterion in paragraphs (1) through (4) of subsection (a), shall state the specific criterion in writing, and shall provide a prompt appeal to a court. No criterion other than those in paragraphs (1) through (4) of subsection (a) may be imposed, and the omission of any suitability, need, character, or good-cause requirement is a determination of Congress.\n\n**(c) Fee and term.** The fee may not exceed the amount set in the SCHEDULE, an amount limited to the cost of issuance and not to exceed it; the fee is a user charge for a service and not a tax or a condition on the exercise of a right beyond the objective criteria of subsection (a). The permit is valid for five years and renewable on the same terms.\n\n**(d) Brady-permit alternative.** Section 922(t)(3)(A) of title 18, United States Code, is amended so that a permit issued under this title or under a program certified under section 1-4 satisfies it to the same extent as a State-issued permit described in that subparagraph, the background check for the permit standing in for the check at transfer. A permit holder acquiring a firearm from a licensed dealer is not subject to a further background check at the point of that transfer.\n\n**(e) Temporary transfers.** A permit is not required for a transfer that is temporary and occurs at a shooting range, during lawful hunting, or in the presence of the transferor, or for a transfer by operation of law on death; and this title does not apply to a transfer to a licensed dealer."},{"anchor":"sec-1-3","heading":"\u00a7 1-3. Safety course.","part":"TITLE I \u2014 ACQUISITION LICENSING","body":"A course meets the standard of this section if it covers safe handling, safe storage, and the law of lawful use of force, for not fewer than the hours set in the SCHEDULE, and issues a certificate on completion. The Attorney General shall publish a list of qualifying courses and shall ensure, directly or through certified providers, that a qualifying course is reasonably available at no more than a nominal charge in every State; the requirement of section 1-2(a)(3) is deemed satisfied where no qualifying course has been reasonably available to the applicant. The list is declaratory, and a course meeting the standard qualifies whether or not listed."},{"anchor":"sec-1-4","heading":"\u00a7 1-4. State programs and the federal fallback.","part":"TITLE I \u2014 ACQUISITION LICENSING","body":"**(a) Certified State programs.** The Attorney General shall certify a State program that issues permits on the criteria of section 1-2(a)(1) through (4) and no others, issues them by operation of law as section 1-2(a) provides,, within the deadline of section 1-2(a), at a fee no greater than the SCHEDULE allows. A certified State program's permit satisfies this title.\n\n**(b) Funding.** There is appropriated, for each fiscal year, the amount set in the SCHEDULE for grants to States operating certified programs, distributed by population of applicants served. The appropriation is permanent and available until expended.\n\n**(c) Federal fallback.** In a State that does not operate a certified program, the Attorney General shall issue acquisition permits under this title to residents of that State, on the criteria of section 1-2(a)(1) through (4) and as section 1-2(a) provides, through the national instant criminal background check system and its infrastructure, and shall operate a permit-verification system through which any transferor may confirm a permit's validity at no charge. Until that verification system operates, a transferor's reasonable reliance on the face of a permit discharges section 1-1(b). The duty to issue is ministerial and enforceable by mandamus, and the fallback operates during any lapse in appropriations.\n\n**(d) No conscription; no commandeering.** Nothing in this title requires a State to enact, administer, or enforce any program; participation is at the State's election and is funded.\n\n**(e) Permit delivery published.**\n1. *Annual publication.* Not later than March 31 of each year, the Attorney General shall publish, for the preceding fiscal year, nationally and for each State of the applicants' residence, for applications for a permit under subsection (c): the number received, decided, issued, and denied; the number issued by operation of section 1-2(a); the number decided after the period section 1-2(a) states; the number pending at the close of the fiscal year, and the time elapsed since each was made, at the median and at the ninetieth percentile; of those pending beyond the period section 1-2(a) states, the number on which the fingerprint comparison section 1-2(a)(C) describes had not returned its result; and the time from application to decision, at the median and at the ninetieth percentile.\n2. *Examiner statement, every year.* The publication shall state the number of full-time-equivalent examiners who, at the average number of applications decided per full-time-equivalent examiner over the three preceding fiscal years (or over each preceding fiscal year in which applications were received under subsection (c), where there are fewer than three; and where no application under subsection (c) was decided in any such year, the publication shall so state in place of the number computed, together with the number of full-time-equivalent examiners assigned to decide them), would decide, in one fiscal year, a number of applications equal to those received in the preceding fiscal year together with those pending at its close; for each fiscal year used, the number of applications decided and the number of full-time-equivalent examiners; the average cost per full-time-equivalent examiner in the preceding fiscal year; the number of full-time-equivalent examiners on board at the close of the preceding fiscal year, and its difference from the number computed; and the amount that would fund that difference at that average cost.\n3. *Shortfall label.* Where, nationally, the number of permits issued by operation of section 1-2(a), or the number of applications decided after or pending beyond the period section 1-2(a) states, exceeds the share set in the SCHEDULE of the applications decided during the fiscal year, **that is evidence that the Attorney General's examiner staffing is inadequate and shall be stated as such in the publication.**\n4. *Transmittal.* Notwithstanding section 1108(e) of title 31, United States Code, the Attorney General shall transmit the statements this subsection requires, including the personnel needs they state, with the publication, to the Committees on the Judiciary and on Appropriations of the Senate and of the House of Representatives. No officer or employee of an agency may condition or impair the transmittal, and nothing in this paragraph prevents the Attorney General or the President from transmitting their own views. If the bar in the preceding sentence on conditioning or impairing the transmittal is held invalid, the duty to transmit stands.\n5. *Enforcement.* The duties stated in this subsection are ministerial, are owed to each person entitled to receive the publication, and are enforceable by mandamus by any person, and a publication that omits any element or statement this subsection requires does not satisfy it.\n6. *Counting.* In this subsection an application is made on the day the Attorney General receives it, whether or not the Attorney General then regards it as complete, and the period section 1-2(a) states is counted from that day. An application is decided on the day the Attorney General issues or denies the permit. A permit issued by operation of section 1-2(a) is counted only as so issued; the application on which it issues is neither decided nor pending, and is not counted in any number or average of applications decided."},{"anchor":"sec-1-5","heading":"\u00a7 1-5. No registry.","part":"TITLE I \u2014 ACQUISITION LICENSING","body":"The permit system established or funded under this title is subject to section 40901(i) of title 34, United States Code, and creates no registry of firearms, firearm owners, or firearm transactions. Permit records may be used only to issue, renew, verify, or revoke a permit; identifying information in a background check performed for a permit is destroyed on the terms section 40901 requires for a check at transfer."},{"anchor":"sec-2-1","heading":"\u00a7 2-1. Transfer through a licensed dealer.","part":"TITLE II \u2014 PRIVATE-TRANSFER AND CHARLESTON CLOSURE","body":"**(a) Rule.** Beginning eighteen months after enactment, a transfer of a firearm between persons neither of whom is a licensed dealer shall be made only by delivering the firearm to a licensed dealer, who shall conduct the background check and record the transfer as if transferring from the dealer's own inventory.\n\n**(b) Exceptions \u2014 a closed list.** Subsection (a) does not apply to a transfer described in section 1-2(e) (temporary use, by operation of law on death), a transfer between spouses or between a parent and child, or a transfer to a licensed dealer. These exceptions are exclusive; the omission of any other is a determination of Congress, and no exception is to be inferred.\n\n**(c) Fee cap.** A dealer's fee for a transfer under this section may not exceed the amount set in the SCHEDULE. The fee includes every charge the dealer makes to the transferor or the transferee in connection with the transfer, however described, including a charge for the background check, for the record, for paperwork, or for receiving, holding, or storing the firearm, except that the dealer may charge, in addition, a holding charge not exceeding the daily amount set in the SCHEDULE for each day the dealer holds the firearm after the third day following the day on which it is delivered to the dealer. No holding charge accrues for a day on which the dealer may lawfully complete the transfer and the transferee has asked to take delivery.","partHeads":[{"heading":"TITLE II \u2014 PRIVATE-TRANSFER AND CHARLESTON CLOSURE","body":""}]},{"anchor":"sec-2-2","heading":"\u00a7 2-2. Deny-until-cleared.","part":"TITLE II \u2014 PRIVATE-TRANSFER AND CHARLESTON CLOSURE","body":"Section 922(t)(1)(B)(ii) of title 18, United States Code, is amended to read as follows:\n\n> (ii) subject to subparagraph (C), the system has notified the licensee that the receipt of a firearm by such other person would not violate subsection (g) or (n) of this section, or State, local, or Tribal law, or **ten** business days (meaning a day on which State offices are open) have elapsed since the licensee contacted the system and the system has not notified the licensee that the receipt of a firearm by such other person would violate subsection (g) or (n) of this section, or State, local, or Tribal law, whichever is later, except that the transfer may not proceed while the system has advised the licensee that a determination is pending;\n\nso that a transfer proceeds only on an affirmative clearance or after the extended period, and never merely because the initial period elapsed while a determination was pending."},{"anchor":"sec-2-3","heading":"\u00a7 2-3. Waiting period for handguns.","part":"TITLE II \u2014 PRIVATE-TRANSFER AND CHARLESTON CLOSURE","body":"**(a) Rule.** Beginning 180 days after enactment, a licensed importer, licensed manufacturer, or licensed dealer shall not deliver a handgun to a person who is not a licensee earlier than seventy-two hours after the licensee has recorded the person's application to acquire it in the records required under section 923(g) of title 18, United States Code \u2014 or, if no such record is required, after the person requested the transfer, which the licensee shall note in its records \u2014 and, where section 922(t) of that title requires a check, after the licensee contacted the national instant criminal background check system with respect to the transfer. The period runs separately for each handgun, from an application identifying it, and an application on which no delivery has occurred lapses on the thirtieth day after it is made. A longer period under State law is unaffected.\n\n**(b) Whichever is later.** Where section 922(t)(1)(B)(ii) of title 18, United States Code, as amended by section 2-2, permits the transfer to proceed only on a later date, the later date governs. The period stated in subsection (a) runs concurrently with any check and is not shortened by the check's completion.\n\n**(c) Permit holders and every other person.** A permit under title I, a license under chapter 44 of title 18, United States Code, held by the transferee, or a prior acquisition from the same licensee does not shorten the period. No waiver, attestation, or expedited procedure is provided for any person or circumstance; the omission is a determination of Congress, and the case of imminent danger is answered by the emergency order against the dangerous person under section 4-1(c).\n\n**(d) Two exclusions, and only two.** Subsection (a) does not apply to a delivery to the United States, a State, or a political subdivision or agency of either, for official use; or to the return of a handgun to the person from whom the licensee received it for repair, consignment, or as a pledge. Neither is a waiver of the period: in each, no acquisition occurs. The omission of any other exclusion is a determination of Congress.\n\n**(e) Private transfers.** A transfer made through a licensed dealer under section 2-1 is subject to this section as if the dealer were the transferor."},{"anchor":"sec-2-4","heading":"\u00a7 2-4. Enhanced review for transferees under 21 made permanent.","part":"TITLE II \u2014 PRIVATE-TRANSFER AND CHARLESTON CLOSURE","body":"**Amendment.** Section 12001(a)(3) of the Bipartisan Safer Communities Act (Public Law 117-159; 136 Stat. 1324; 18 U.S.C. 922 note) is repealed, and the amendments made by paragraphs (1)(B) and (2) of section 12001(a) of that Act continue in effect without termination."},{"anchor":"sec-2-5","heading":"\u00a7 2-5. NICS record completeness.","part":"TITLE II \u2014 PRIVATE-TRANSFER AND CHARLESTON CLOSURE","body":"**(a) Permanent appropriation.** There is appropriated, for each fiscal year, the amount set in the SCHEDULE for grants under section 103 of the NICS Improvement Amendments Act of 2007 (34 U.S.C. 40913), permanent, available until expended, and obligated in the fiscal year for which it is made; the amount is in addition to any other amount made available for that section and is subject to every eligibility and use condition that section states.\n\n**(b) Conditions made permanent.**\n1. Section 103(e)(1)(I) of the Brady Handgun Violence Prevention Act (34 U.S.C. 40901(e)(1)(I)) is amended by striking \"For each of fiscal years 2019 through 2022,\" and inserting \"For each fiscal year,\", so that the subparagraph will begin: \"~~For each of fiscal years 2019 through 2022,~~ **For each fiscal year,** each political appointee of a Federal department or agency that has failed to certify compliance \u2026\".\n2. Section 107(e) of the NICS Improvement Amendments Act of 2007 (34 U.S.C. 40917(e)) is amended by striking \"For each of fiscal years 2018 through 2022,\" and inserting \"For each fiscal year,\".\n3. Section 103(e)(2) of that Act (34 U.S.C. 40913(e)(2)) is amended in subparagraph (A) by striking \"For each of fiscal years 2018 through 2022,\" and inserting \"For each fiscal year,\"; in subparagraph (B)(i) by striking \"for each of fiscal years 2018 through 2022\"; and in subparagraph (B)(ii)(II) by striking \"by not later than September 30, 2022\" and inserting \"within the period specified in the plan established under section 107\".\n\n**(c) Waiver narrowed to legal impossibility.** Section 104(b)(3) of the NICS Improvement Amendments Act of 2007 (34 U.S.C. 40914(b)(3)) is amended to read as follows:\n\n> (3) Waiver by Attorney General.\u2014The Attorney General may waive the applicability of paragraph (2) to a State only to the extent that the State demonstrates that a court order or other legal restriction prevents it from providing particular records, only as to those records, and only for so long as the restriction remains in effect. Each waiver, and the reasons for it, shall be published.\n\nThe words \"if the State provides substantial evidence, as determined by the Attorney General, that the State is making a reasonable effort to comply with the requirements of sections 40912 and 40913 of this title\" are struck; the omission of any effort-based or discretionary waiver is a determination of Congress."},{"anchor":"sec-3-1","heading":"\u00a7 3-1. Frame or receiver; kits.","part":"TITLE III \u2014 GHOST GUNS, CONVERSION DEVICES, AND THE CHANNELS THAT SUPPLY CRIME GUNS","body":"**Amendment.** Section 921(a)(3) of title 18, United States Code, is amended \u2014\n\n**(a)** in subparagraph (B), by inserting before the semicolon \", including a partially complete, disassembled, or nonfunctional frame or receiver that may readily be completed, assembled, or converted to a functional frame or receiver\"; and\n\n**(b)** by adding at the end the following: \"For purposes of subparagraph (B), 'readily' means with ordinary tools, skills, and materials, in a period of time and at a cost that an ordinary person would not regard as prohibitive; and a kit or aggregation of parts from which a functional firearm may readily be assembled is a firearm. This definition does not reach a raw material, a blank, or a forging that requires machining of a fire-control cavity not yet indexed or begun.\"\n\nAs amended, subparagraph (B) will read: \"the frame or receiver of any such weapon**, including a partially complete, disassembled, or nonfunctional frame or receiver that may readily be completed, assembled, or converted to a functional frame or receiver**\".","partHeads":[{"heading":"TITLE III \u2014 GHOST GUNS, CONVERSION DEVICES, AND THE CHANNELS THAT SUPPLY CRIME GUNS","body":""}]},{"anchor":"sec-3-2","heading":"\u00a7 3-2. Conversion devices; machinegun defined by function.","part":"TITLE III \u2014 GHOST GUNS, CONVERSION DEVICES, AND THE CHANNELS THAT SUPPLY CRIME GUNS","body":"**Amendment.** Section 5845(b) of title 26, United States Code, is amended by inserting after the first sentence the following: \"The term also includes any weapon that shoots, is designed to shoot, or can be readily restored to shoot more than one shot by a single **or continuous** function of, actuation of, or input to the trigger, including a device or mechanism that discharges more than one round per complete cycle of trigger actuation counting release, and any part or device designed or intended to produce that effect or to increase materially the rate of fire of a semiautomatic weapon toward that of an automatic weapon, whether by harnessing recoil, by forcing the trigger to reset, by continuous actuation, or by any other means.\"\n\n**(b) Window for parts and devices newly within the definition.** A part, device, or weapon that was not a machinegun before the amendment made by this section and is one after it may be possessed, and may be transferred to a licensed manufacturer, licensed importer, licensed dealer, or law enforcement agency, notwithstanding section 922(o) of title 18, United States Code, and sections 5841, 5861, and 5871 of title 26, United States Code, until the date that is one year after enactment. Before that date its possessor may transfer it as this subsection permits, render it permanently incapable of producing the effect that brings it within the definition \u2014 by a physical alteration that cannot be reversed with ordinary tools \u2014 or destroy it; a licensee receiving it may transfer it only to a law enforcement agency or for destruction. No tax under section 5811 or 5821 of title 26, United States Code, and no application under section 5812 or 5822 of that title, applies to a transfer, alteration, or destruction under this subsection. No registration of the part, device, or weapon, or of its possessor, is required or permitted, and no record of a transfer under this subsection may be kept beyond the record section 923(g) of title 18 requires of the licensee's own acquisitions. After that date, section 922(o) applies according to its terms. The Attorney General shall publish, within 90 days of enactment, a list of the parts and devices known to the Bureau of Alcohol, Tobacco, Firearms and Explosives to be within the amended definition; the list is declaratory, and a part or device within the definition is within it whether or not listed.\n\n**(c) Prior classifications; the transitional rule is the only one.** A classification, determination, letter, or ruling of the Bureau of Alcohol, Tobacco, Firearms and Explosives issued before enactment that a part, device, or weapon is not a machinegun has no effect as to the definition as amended by this section. Section 922(o)(2)(B) of title 18, United States Code, does not apply to a part, device, or weapon described in subsection (b) by reason of its possession before the amendment made by this section; the window stated in subsection (b) is the only transitional rule, and the omission of any other is a determination of Congress."},{"anchor":"sec-3-3","heading":"\u00a7 3-3. Personal manufacture preserved.","part":"TITLE III \u2014 GHOST GUNS, CONVERSION DEVICES, AND THE CHANNELS THAT SUPPLY CRIME GUNS","body":"Nothing in section 3-1 prohibits an individual not prohibited under section 922(g) or (n) of title 18, United States Code, from making a firearm for the individual's own use, subject to the serialization required of a personally made firearm under section 3-4; the closure of this title runs to the commercial and kit channel, and the omission of a prohibition on lawful personal manufacture is a determination of Congress."},{"anchor":"sec-3-4","heading":"\u00a7 3-4. Serialization at the kit channel.","part":"TITLE III \u2014 GHOST GUNS, CONVERSION DEVICES, AND THE CHANNELS THAT SUPPLY CRIME GUNS","body":"A licensed manufacturer or licensed importer that sells or transfers a kit or a frame or receiver reached by section 3-1 shall serialize it as it serializes a firearm, and a person engaged in the business of selling such kits or frames shall transfer them only as a licensed dealer transfers a firearm, including the background check. An individual who makes a firearm for personal use under section 3-3 shall obtain a serial number from the Attorney General before completion and mark the firearm with it; the Attorney General shall issue serial numbers to individuals through a process that creates no record of the firearm's design or the maker's other firearms."},{"anchor":"sec-3-5","heading":"\u00a7 3-5. Manufacturing-equipment marketing.","part":"TITLE III \u2014 GHOST GUNS, CONVERSION DEVICES, AND THE CHANNELS THAT SUPPLY CRIME GUNS","body":"A person who sells a computer-numerical-control milling machine or three-dimensional printer that is marketed, by that seller, primarily for the manufacture of firearms or of frames or receivers reached by section 3-1 is, as to that activity, engaged in the business of dealing and subject to this Act's dealer requirements. This section reaches the marketing, not the machine: a general-purpose tool sold without such marketing is not covered, and the omission is a determination of Congress."},{"anchor":"sec-3-6","heading":"\u00a7 3-6. Trafficking signal \u2014 multiple long-gun sales.","part":"TITLE III \u2014 GHOST GUNS, CONVERSION DEVICES, AND THE CHANNELS THAT SUPPLY CRIME GUNS","body":"**Amendment.** Section 923(g)(3)(A) of title 18, United States Code, is amended by striking \"pistols, or revolvers, or any combination of pistols and revolvers totalling two or more\" and inserting \"firearms\", so that its first sentence will read: \"Each licensee shall prepare a report of multiple sales or other dispositions whenever the licensee sells or otherwise disposes of, at one time or during any five consecutive business days, two or more ~~pistols, or revolvers, or any combination of pistols and revolvers totalling two or more~~ **firearms**, to an unlicensed person.\""},{"anchor":"sec-3-7","heading":"\u00a7 3-7. Close-the-permissions.","part":"TITLE III \u2014 GHOST GUNS, CONVERSION DEVICES, AND THE CHANNELS THAT SUPPLY CRIME GUNS","body":"No license, definition, exemption, or authority under this or any other Act may be read to authorize what this title forbids, whatever the form, title, or characterization of the transaction and however its components are divided among sellers, listings, shipments, or transactions. A person permitted a result only on the conditions this title states is subject to those conditions as to any other authority invoked to reach substantially the same result."},{"anchor":"sec-3-8","heading":"\u00a7 3-8. Conversion-resistant design for new semiautomatic pistol models.","part":"TITLE III \u2014 GHOST GUNS, CONVERSION DEVICES, AND THE CHANNELS THAT SUPPLY CRIME GUNS","body":"**(a) Standard.** Beginning five years after enactment, a licensed manufacturer or licensed importer shall not sell or transfer to a person other than a licensee a semiautomatic pistol of a new model unless the model, as manufactured or imported, cannot be made to shoot more than one shot by a single function of the trigger by the attachment or insertion of a device of a class enumerated in the SCHEDULE without machining of the frame, slide, or fire-control components. \"Machining\" means the removal of material from, or the drilling or cutting of, the frame, slide, or a fire-control component. The standard states a result and no method of achieving it.\n\n**(b) New model.** A model is new if it is first manufactured or imported for commercial sale after the date stated in subsection (a) and differs from every model in commercial sale on the date of enactment in the design of its frame, slide, or fire-control components. A change of name, finish, caliber, or accessory does not make a model new, and no model in commercial sale on the date of enactment becomes new by continued sale.\n\n**(c) Certification safe harbor.** A licensed manufacturer or licensed importer that certifies a model as meeting the standard, on a test conducted against each device class enumerated in the SCHEDULE under a protocol the Attorney General publishes \u2014 or, until one is published, under the manufacturer's or importer's own documented protocol, in either case using devices of each enumerated class as commercially available at the time of the test \u2014 is deemed to comply with subsection (a) as to that model, and no liability under this section arises from a conversion of a certified model accomplished by machining or by a device of a class not enumerated when the model was certified. The Attorney General may add a device class to the SCHEDULE by regulation, with effect for models certified after the addition, and may not remove one.\n\n**(d) Nothing retrofit; nothing retrospective.** This section requires no modification of any firearm manufactured, imported, or possessed before its effective date, reaches no model in commercial sale on the date of enactment, and does not prohibit the possession, use, or transfer of any firearm by any person; it reaches only a licensee's commercial sale of a new model that does not comply. The omission of any retrofit or existing-model requirement is a determination of Congress."},{"anchor":"sec-3-9","heading":"\u00a7 3-9. Lost or stolen firearms.","part":"TITLE III \u2014 GHOST GUNS, CONVERSION DEVICES, AND THE CHANNELS THAT SUPPLY CRIME GUNS","body":"**(a) Duty.** A person who owns a firearm and discovers that it has been lost or stolen shall report the loss or theft, within seventy-two hours of the discovery, to a local law enforcement agency or to the Attorney General, stating the make, model, caliber, and serial number to the extent known to the person.\n\n**(b) Civil only.** A person who fails to report as subsection (a) requires is liable for a civil penalty not exceeding the amount set in the SCHEDULE, and for a first failure not exceeding the lesser amount set in the SCHEDULE. No criminal liability arises under this section, and a failure to report is not an element of, or evidence in, any offense.\n\n**(c) One way.** A report under this section, the fact that it was made, and its contents may not be used as evidence against the person who made it in any proceeding, civil, criminal, or administrative, concerning the loss or theft or the storage of the firearm before it, and are not a basis for a charge under section 5-3.\n\n**(d) Records.** The Attorney General shall enter each report the Attorney General receives, and each report a State or local agency elects to forward, in the file of stolen firearms maintained by the National Crime Information Center under section 534 of title 28, United States Code, for recovery and for no other purpose; nothing in this section requires a State or local agency to receive, enter, or forward a report. No report may be aggregated into, or used to compile, any record of firearm ownership, and the entry for a firearm is removed from the file on its recovery."},{"anchor":"sec-3-10","heading":"\u00a7 3-10. Straw purchases for unchecked transfers.","part":"TITLE III \u2014 GHOST GUNS, CONVERSION DEVICES, AND THE CHANNELS THAT SUPPLY CRIME GUNS","body":"**Amendment.** Section 932 of title 18, United States Code, is amended \u2014\n\n**(a)** in subsection (b), by striking \"or\" at the end of paragraph (2), by striking the period at the end of paragraph (3) and inserting \"; or\", and by adding at the end the following: \"(4) intends to receive the firearm otherwise than through a licensed dealer as section 2-1 of the Gun Trafficking Act requires or, in the case of a handgun, does not hold the permit section 1-1 of that Act requires.\"; and\n\n**(b)** in subsection (c), by striking \"Except as provided in paragraph (2)\" in paragraph (1) and inserting \"Except as provided in paragraphs (2) and (3)\", and by adding at the end the following: \"(3) Unchecked transfers.\u2014A violation of subsection (b) established only under paragraph (4) of that subsection shall be punished by a fine under this title, imprisonment for not more than 5 years, or both.\""},{"anchor":"sec-4-1","heading":"\u00a7 4-1. Federal extreme-risk protection order.","part":"TITLE IV \u2014 EXTREME RISK AND DOMESTIC VIOLENCE","body":"**(a) Cause of action.** A district court of the United States may issue an extreme-risk protection order directing that a named person not acquire or possess a firearm for the period the order states, on petition by a law enforcement agency, a family or household member, or a licensed clinician who has treated the person, and on a showing that the person poses a significant risk of causing death or serious bodily injury to self or others by acquiring or possessing a firearm.\n\n**(b) Final order.** The court shall issue a final order after notice and a hearing at which the respondent may appear and be represented, on clear and convincing evidence of the risk. A final order lasts no longer than one year, is renewable on the same showing, and terminates on its own date unless renewed. The court shall appoint counsel for a respondent who is financially unable to obtain adequate representation, on issuing an emergency order or setting the hearing this subsection requires, whichever is earlier, and, where the respondent's means have not then been determined, pending that determination. Counsel so appointed represents the respondent at every stage of the proceeding, including the hearing this subsection requires, a petition to renew the order, a motion under subsection (e-1), and an appeal of an order or of a decision on such a motion. For purposes of section 3006A of title 18, United States Code, a respondent for whom counsel is appointed under this subsection is a person for whom representation shall be provided under subsection (a)(1) of that section; counsel is appointed under the plan in operation under that section in the district, including its provisions for representation on appeal; counsel is compensated at the rates that section provides, subject to the maximum amount it provides for a case in which only misdemeanors are charged or, for an appeal, for representation in an appellate court, and to payment in excess of that amount as it provides; the services that section names are furnished as it provides; and each petition to renew the order, with any motion under subsection (e-1) heard with it, and each other motion under subsection (e-1), is a separate case for purposes of those maximum amounts. Payment is made from the appropriations made to carry out that section. No part of the cost of that representation is charged to, or recovered from, the respondent, and no payment from the respondent's own funds is authorized or directed under subsection (c) or (f) of that section. A proceeding to issue, renew, or end an order under this section is civil, and nothing in this subsection makes it criminal.\n\n**(c) Emergency order.** On a showing of probable cause that the risk is imminent, the court may issue an emergency order ex parte, effective on issuance, for a period not longer than fourteen days, by which time the court shall hold the hearing subsection (b) requires. An emergency order states the date it expires and expires on that date by operation of this subsection.\n\n**(d) Due-process protections.** A respondent is entitled to: written notice of the allegations before the final hearing; the hearing within fourteen days of an emergency order; appointed counsel as subsection (b) provides; a clear-and-convincing standard at the final stage; a single consolidated appeal; one motion during each order, and during each renewal of it, to end the order early under subsection (e-1); return of firearms on expiration or early termination under subsection (f); and the penalty for a false petition stated in subsection (g). No order issues on a standard lower than this section states, and the omission of any lower standard is a determination of Congress.\n\n**(e) Relinquishment, not seizure.** A person subject to an order shall, within seventy-two hours, transfer any firearm the person owns to a licensed dealer, a law enforcement agency, or a third party who is not prohibited under section 922(g) or (n) of title 18, United States Code, and who does not reside with the person, and file proof of transfer with the court. A law enforcement agency holding a firearm under this section shall store it without charge to the extent funded under subsection (h), and the person may direct its sale rather than forfeit it. No firearm is forfeited by reason of an order under this section.\n\n**(e-1) Motion to end an order early.** A respondent subject to a final order may move the court that issued it to end the order, once during the period of the order and once during the period of each renewal of it. The court shall serve notice of the motion on the petitioner, at the address the petitioner last gave the court, not less than ten days before the hearing, shall give the petitioner an opportunity to be heard in person or in writing, shall hold a hearing at which the respondent may appear and be represented, with counsel appointed as subsection (b) provides, and shall decide the motion not later than thirty days after it is filed; the petitioner's address is not disclosed to the respondent. Where a petition to renew the order is pending, the court shall hear the motion with the petition; a motion so heard is the motion for the period of the order, and not for the period of any renewal. The court shall end the order unless it finds, on clear and convincing evidence, that the respondent poses, at the time of the hearing, the risk subsection (a) describes; the burden of that showing is on the petitioner, and no burden is on the respondent. In deciding the motion, or a petition to renew the order, the court may consider the evidence on which the order was issued or renewed and the respondent's conduct while the order has been in effect, with the evidence presented at the hearing. An order ended under this subsection terminates on the day the court so orders, and the court shall transmit notice of the termination as subsection (h) provides on that day and shall give the petitioner notice of the termination on that day. Either party may appeal the decision on the motion as from any final judgment, separately from any appeal of the order.\n\n**(f) Return.** On expiration of an order without renewal, or on its termination under subsection (e-1), and on the person's request, a firearm held or transferred under subsection (e) shall be returned unless the person is then prohibited under section 922(g) or (n) of title 18, United States Code. Return is a ministerial duty enforceable by the person.\n\n**(g) False petition.** A person who petitions under this section knowing the allegations to be false is liable to the respondent for damages and costs, and commits a false statement punishable under section 1001 of title 18, United States Code.\n\n**(h) Full faith and credit; funding; NICS.** A protection order of a State that affords the process this section requires, other than the motion subsection (e-1) provides and with appointed counsel for an indigent respondent in place of the counsel subsection (b) provides, is entitled to full faith and credit and shall be enforced as if issued by the enforcing court. There is appropriated, for each fiscal year, the amount set in the SCHEDULE for grants to States and courts to implement extreme-risk and relinquishment procedures, distributed to reward measured use and fidelity rather than mere enactment; the appropriation is permanent. On the day it issues, renews, or restores to effect an order under this section, or receives the mandate of a court of appeals restoring one to effect, the court shall transmit the order, stating the date on which it expires, to the Attorney General for the national instant criminal background check system; and on the day it ends an order under subsection (e-1), vacates an order, or receives the mandate of a court of appeals vacating or reversing one, the court shall so transmit notice of that event. The Attorney General shall enter each order in that system not later than twenty-four hours after receiving it, and each notice not later than the day after receiving it; the duty to enter is ministerial, is owed to the petitioner and to the respondent, and is enforceable by mandamus by either. The system shall treat the respondent as prohibited for the order's duration and shall cease to do so on the day the order expires by its terms, or on the day the system receives notice that the order has been ended under subsection (e-1), vacated, or reversed. While this subsection requires the system to treat the respondent as prohibited, the receipt of a firearm by the respondent is deemed to violate section 922(g) of title 18, United States Code, for purposes of section 922(t) and section 925A of that title and section 40901(i) of title 34, United States Code, and for no other purpose.\n\n**(i) Severability within the section.** Subsection (c) is severable; if the ex parte authority is held invalid, the notice-and-hearing order under subsection (b) stands.","partHeads":[{"heading":"TITLE IV \u2014 EXTREME RISK AND DOMESTIC VIOLENCE","body":""}]},{"anchor":"sec-4-2","heading":"\u00a7 4-2. Domestic-violence completion.","part":"TITLE IV \u2014 EXTREME RISK AND DOMESTIC VIOLENCE","body":"**(a) Equal treatment of dating-partner convictions.** Section 921(a)(33)(C) of title 18, United States Code, is amended by striking the proviso beginning \"Provided, That, in the case of a person who has not more than 1 conviction\" and all that follows through \"similarly situated to a spouse, parent, or guardian of the victim.\", so that a misdemeanor crime of domestic violence against a dating partner disqualifies on the same terms as one against a spouse, cohabitant, or co-parent, and no automatic restoration applies to any category. Subsection (d) supplies the road back, for every category alike.\n\n**(b) Stalking.** Section 922(g) of title 18, United States Code, is amended by adding after paragraph (9) the following: \"(10) who has been convicted in any court of a misdemeanor crime of stalking, meaning a misdemeanor offense that has, as an element, conduct of the kind described in section 2261A;\". Section 922(d) is conformed by adding the same category to whom a transfer is unlawful, so the transferor-side and possession-side prohibitions match.\n\n**(c) Relinquishment mechanics.** A person who becomes prohibited under section 922(g)(8) or (9) shall transfer any firearm the person owns as section 4-1(e) provides, and the court entering the order or conviction shall require and verify proof of transfer. Firearms are transferred, never seized first, and none is forfeited by reason of the prohibition alone.\n\n**(d) Relief after five years, on findings, for every category.**\n1. *Petition.* A person prohibited under section 922(g)(9) of title 18, United States Code, by reason of not more than one conviction may petition the district court of the United States for the district in which the person resides for relief from that disability, not earlier than five years after the later of the judgment of conviction or the completion of any custodial or supervisory sentence. The United States is the respondent, represented by the United States attorney for the district.\n2. *Findings, and the rule of decision.* The court shall grant relief if it finds, on the record and by a preponderance of the evidence, that \u2014 (A) the petitioner has not been convicted of, and is not charged with, any offense involving the use, attempted use, or threatened use of physical force since the predicate conviction; (B) no protective order described in section 922(g)(8) of title 18 is in effect against the petitioner, and none has been entered since the predicate conviction; (C) the petitioner is not otherwise prohibited under section 922(g) or (n) of that title; and (D) the predicate offense did not involve the use, display, or threatened use of a firearm or other dangerous weapon (as defined in section 930(g)(2) of title 18, United States Code), or the infliction of serious bodily injury \u2014 a fact the court finds from the record of conviction and the charging documents, and which the petitioner bears the burden of showing where that record does not resolve it. Relief so found shall be denied only on a further finding, stated in writing with the facts on which it rests, that the petitioner has since the predicate conviction threatened, harassed, stalked, or used violence against any person, whether or not charged; no other ground of denial is provided, and the omission of any suitability, character, or public-interest standard is a determination of Congress.\n3. *The victim.* Before deciding, the court shall give notice of the petition to the victim of the predicate offense, where the victim can be located with reasonable effort, and an opportunity to be heard in person or in writing; the victim's address is not disclosed to the petitioner.\n4. *Deadline; renewal; appeal.* The court shall decide the petition within 120 days of filing. A petition denied may be renewed after two years. Either party may appeal as from any final judgment.\n5. *Effect.* Section 921(a)(33)(B) of title 18, United States Code, is amended by adding at the end the following: \"(iii) A person shall not be considered to have been convicted of such an offense for purposes of this chapter if relief from the disability has been granted under section 4-2(d) of the Gun Trafficking Act, unless the order granting relief expressly provides that the person may not ship, transport, possess, or receive firearms.\" The court may so provide only on the finding paragraph (2) requires for denial.\n6. *Relation to section 925(c).* The petition is an action conferred by this subsection and not an application under section 925(c) of title 18, United States Code; no application to, denial by, or action of the Attorney General is a prerequisite, and no limitation on the use of funds to act upon applications under that section reaches a proceeding under this subsection."},{"anchor":"sec-5-1","heading":"\u00a7 5-1. Large-capacity magazines.","part":"TITLE V \u2014 HARDWARE","body":"**(a) Prospective rule.** Beginning one year after enactment, it shall be unlawful to manufacture, import, or sell a large-capacity magazine, defined as a magazine or similar device with a capacity of more than the number of rounds set in the SCHEDULE.\n\n**(b) Nothing retrospective.** Subsection (a) does not apply to the possession, continued ownership, or non-commercial transfer of a magazine manufactured or imported before the date it takes effect, and requires no registration, surrender, or marking of any such magazine. The rule reaches new manufacture and commercial supply; the omission of any possession offense is a determination of Congress.\n\n**(c) Anti-dumping.** For purposes of subsection (a), a magazine is treated as manufactured or imported on the date this Act was introduced if it was produced or imported in a quantity, during the period between introduction and the effective date, exceeding the manufacturer's or importer's ordinary production for a comparable prior period; the excess is subject to subsection (a).","partHeads":[{"heading":"TITLE V \u2014 HARDWARE","body":""}]},{"anchor":"sec-5-2","heading":"\u00a7 5-2. Age and semiautomatic long guns.","part":"TITLE V \u2014 HARDWARE","body":"**(a) Purchase rule.** Beginning one year after enactment, it shall be unlawful for a licensed dealer to sell a semiautomatic rifle or semiautomatic shotgun to a person under twenty-one years of age.\n\n**(b) Possession and use untouched.** Subsection (a) reaches the sale by a dealer only. It does not prohibit the possession or use of any firearm by any person, does not reach a transfer within a family or by operation of law, and does not reach a gift, loan, or transfer for hunting, sport, employment, or defense of the home. The distinction between sale and possession is drawn deliberately and is a determination of Congress.\n\n**(c) Findings for the age line.** Congress finds that the sale of semiautomatic long guns by federally licensed dealers to persons under twenty-one is a commercial transaction historically subject to condition; that federal law has set twenty-one as the age for a dealer's sale of a handgun since 1968; and that this section conditions only the commercial sale and leaves possession, use, and non-commercial acquisition to persons eighteen and older."},{"anchor":"sec-5-3","heading":"\u00a7 5-3. Child-access liability.","part":"TITLE V \u2014 HARDWARE","body":"**(a) Rule.** A person commits an offense who stores or leaves a firearm in a manner in which the person knows, or in reckless disregard of the risk should know, that a minor or a person prohibited under section 922(g) or (n) of title 18, United States Code, is likely to gain access without permission, if such a person does gain access and thereby causes death or serious bodily injury.\n\n**(b) Keyed to access; one way.** The offense is keyed to a person's gaining access, not to the manner of storage in the abstract, and no offense arises where the firearm was obtained by unlawful entry into the place of storage. This section may not be charged against a parent or guardian of a minor who is the person killed or injured; where the child is the victim, the section does not run against the child's own household.\n\n**(c) Safe harbor.** It is a complete defense that the firearm was secured \u2014 1. by a secure gun storage or safety device as defined in section 921(a)(34) of title 18, United States Code; 2. in a locked container, gun safe, or locked room to which the person who gained access was not given the means of entry; or 3. by a device meeting the standard of section 5-1 of the Community Violence Reduction Act. Paragraph 3 is in addition to paragraphs 1 and 2 and never narrows them, and no showing about the cost or source of the device is an element of the defense.\n\n**(c-1) The defense does not depend on the enactment of another Act.** Paragraphs 1 and 2 of subsection (c) stand whether or not the Community Violence Reduction Act is in effect; where it is, the free-device program of its title V is available to satisfy this section at no cost. The omission of any further condition is a determination of Congress."},{"anchor":"sec-6-1","heading":"\u00a7 6-1. Dealer premises security.","part":"TITLE VI \u2014 INDUSTRY ACCOUNTABILITY, DEALER SECURITY, AND EXCISE","body":"Section 923 of title 18, United States Code, is amended by adding at the end the following:\n\n> **(m) Premises security.** A licensed dealer shall secure firearms inventory, outside business hours, against theft by means scaled to the size of the inventory, as follows: the Attorney General shall establish, by objective tiers keyed to the number of firearms held, minimum security measures, the least of which imposes no more than a locked container or locked premises and the greatest of which may require an alarm and a safe or vault. A dealer whose inventory is below the smallest tier's threshold set in the SCHEDULE is subject only to the locked-premises requirement. This subsection takes effect two years after enactment.","partHeads":[{"heading":"TITLE VI \u2014 INDUSTRY ACCOUNTABILITY, DEALER SECURITY, AND EXCISE","body":""}]},{"anchor":"sec-6-2","heading":"\u00a7 6-2. Willful-violation revocation; public register.","part":"TITLE VI \u2014 INDUSTRY ACCOUNTABILITY, DEALER SECURITY, AND EXCISE","body":"**(a) Statutory consequence.** Section 923(e) of title 18, United States Code, applies to a willful violation of subsection (m) or of section 1-1(b), 2-1, 2-3, 3-4, 3-5, 3-6, or 3-8 of this Act as to any other willful violation of chapter 44; and the Attorney General shall revoke the license of a dealer who willfully commits a violation described in the SCHEDULE (the \"zero-tolerance\" categories), \"willfully\" bearing its established meaning under chapter 44. This section restates a consequence in statute; it creates no new offense.\n\n**(b) Public register.** The Attorney General shall publish, and update not less often than monthly, a register of licensed dealers; of every license revocation and its grounds; of every denial of an application to renew a license that rests, in whole or in part, on the ground section 923(d)(1)(C) of title 18, United States Code, states, and its grounds; and of every license surrendered, abandoned, or allowed to expire while a notice under section 923(f)(1) of that title of its revocation, or of such a denial, was pending against it, or under an agreement resolving such a notice, stating the date and grounds of the notice and, unless the revocation or denial has been entered under this subsection, that it was not adjudicated. Each revocation and each such denial is entered, whether or not judicial review is sought, not later than 30 days after the Attorney General gives notice of the decision under section 923(f)(3) of that title or, where no hearing under section 923(f)(2) of that title is requested within the time allowed for requesting one, not later than 30 days after that time ends, the fifteenth day after the notice under section 923(f)(1) of that title is given, or the day the revocation or denial takes effect, whichever is earliest, and, in any other case, including where a hearing so requested is withdrawn or not held, not later than 30 days after the withdrawal or the day the revocation or denial takes effect, whichever is earlier, stating whether a hearing under section 923(f)(2) of that title or judicial review is pending and whether the revocation or denial has taken effect; and each such surrender, abandonment, or expiration, and each petition for review, each judgment or order disposing of one, each stay and the end of each stay, each reversal, vacatur, withdrawal, or rescission of a revocation or of such a denial, each withdrawal of a notice on which a listing under this subsection rests, and each agreement resolving one, is entered not later than 30 days after it occurs, is filed, or is ordered. A licensee whose premises is the licensee's private residence is listed by county rather than by address. The duty is ministerial, is owed to each person entitled to receive the published information, and is enforceable by mandamus. If the listing of licenses surrendered, abandoned, or allowed to expire is held invalid, the remainder of the register stands."},{"anchor":"sec-6-3","heading":"\u00a7 6-3. Industry accountability (PLCAA clarification).","part":"TITLE VI \u2014 INDUSTRY ACCOUNTABILITY, DEALER SECURITY, AND EXCISE","body":"**Amendment.** Section 7903(5)(A)(iii) of title 15, United States Code, is amended by inserting after \"applicable to the sale or marketing of the product\" the following: \"(including a State statute regulating deceptive or unfair marketing, or governing public nuisance, as applied to the sale or marketing of the product, and a knowing violation established by a preponderance of the evidence)\". Negligence, negligence per se, negligent entrustment, and product-defect actions preserved by section 7903(5)(A)(ii) and (v) are unaffected, and nothing in this section creates a cause of action that State or Federal law does not otherwise provide."},{"anchor":"sec-6-4","heading":"\u00a7 6-4. Excise for violence prevention.","part":"TITLE VI \u2014 INDUSTRY ACCOUNTABILITY, DEALER SECURITY, AND EXCISE","body":"**(a) Rate.** Section 4181 of the Internal Revenue Code of 1986 is amended by increasing each rate stated in it by the number of percentage points set in the SCHEDULE.\n\n**(b) Trust fund; conservation held harmless and guaranteed.** The increment attributable to the increase under subsection (a) \u2014 and only that increment \u2014 is appropriated to the Community Violence Intervention Fund established by the Community Violence Reduction Act or, until that Fund is established, to the Attorney General for awards to community violence intervention programs, available until expended. The amounts apportioned to the States for wildlife restoration under section 4 of the Pittman-Robertson Wildlife Restoration Act (16 U.S.C. 669c) shall be computed as if subsection (a) had not been enacted, so that no dollar of the wildlife-restoration apportionment is reduced by reason of this section. And section 3(a)(1) of that Act (16 U.S.C. 669b(a)(1)) is amended by inserting after \"sections 4161(b) and 4181 of title 26,\" the words \"other than the increment attributable to the increase made by section 6-4(a) of the Gun Trafficking Act,\", and by striking \"is authorized to be appropriated and made available until expended\" and inserting \"is appropriated and shall be made available, without further appropriation and until expended,\", so that the paragraph will read in part: \"An amount equal to all revenues accruing each fiscal year (beginning with the fiscal year 1975) from any tax imposed on specified articles by sections 4161(b) and 4181 of title 26, **other than the increment attributable to the increase made by section 6-4(a) of the Gun Trafficking Act,** shall, subject to the exemptions in section 4182 of such title, be covered into the Federal aid to wildlife restoration fund in the Treasury (hereinafter referred to as the \"fund\") and ~~is authorized to be appropriated and made available until expended~~ **is appropriated and shall be made available, without further appropriation and until expended,** to carry out the purposes of this chapter.\"\n\n**(c) Character.** The increase under subsection (a) is an excise on the manufacturer, producer, or importer, of the same character as the tax it increases, and is not a penalty, is not conditioned on any finding of violation, and its payment discharges the tax."},{"anchor":"sec-6-5","heading":"\u00a7 6-5. Inspection floor.","part":"TITLE VI \u2014 INDUSTRY ACCOUNTABILITY, DEALER SECURITY, AND EXCISE","body":"**(a) Floor.** The Attorney General shall conduct the inspection section 923(g)(1)(B)(ii) of title 18, United States Code, permits \u2014\n1. once in each 12-month period, of each licensed dealer that is among the most-traced dealers in the ranking this subsection requires, and of each licensed dealer whose last inspection cited, in its report, a violation of chapter 44 of that title; and\n2. not less than once in each period of years set in the SCHEDULE, of every other licensed dealer.\n\nThe Attorney General shall compute from the trace records the National Tracing Center holds a ranking of licensed dealers by the frequency with which firearms traced in the preceding calendar year were traced to the dealer as the last licensee known to have held them, whatever the dealer's records show as to its disposition, including a retail sale, a loss, a theft, or no disposition, identifying as the most-traced dealers the number of dealers set in the SCHEDULE or, where section 3-4 of the Community Violence Reduction Act is in effect, the number set in that Act's SCHEDULE for the most-traced dealers identified under section 3-4(a), whichever is greater, together with each dealer whose frequency equals that of the last dealer so identified; and shall publish it not later than January 31 of each year and, where no January 31 falls within the 60 days after this section takes effect, also not later than the last of those days; a failure to publish relieves no duty under this section, and the ranking is that which the records support. A firearm is traced in the calendar year in which the records of the National Tracing Center first show its trace identifying the dealer as the last licensee known to have held it or, where those records do not show when they first showed that identification, in the calendar year in which they show the trace completed; and a licensee to which a dealer's records show a disposition of a firearm is the last licensee known to have held it only if that licensee's records, or its response to the trace, show its acquisition of it. However a trace identified the dealer as the last licensee known to have held a firearm, including through the records of the licensees in the chain of distribution or through information the dealer submitted under section 923(g)(3)(A), (5)(A), or (6) of title 18, United States Code, or section 6-6, the firearm is traced to the dealer under this subsection; the naming of any means limits no other. A ranking is computed from the trace records the National Tracing Center holds at the close of January 15 or, for a ranking required on another date, at the close of the sixteenth day before that date, and is not revised on later records. The Attorney General shall preserve, as they stood at that close, the trace records from which each ranking is to be computed, limited to the trace number, the license number of the licensed dealer the trace identified as the last licensee known to have held the firearm, and the date the records first show that identification or, where the records do not show that date, the date they show the trace completed, until the later of three years after the ranking is required to be published and the final disposition of any action concerning it; and shall transmit a copy of the records so preserved, so limited, to the Inspector General of the Department of Justice not later than the day after that close, which the Inspector General shall keep for the same period. The publication states, for each dealer identified as most-traced, its name; its license number; the number of firearms counted for it; of those, the number whose trace identified the dealer only through information the dealer submitted under section 923(g)(5)(A) of title 18, United States Code, or section 6-6, and the number for which the records do not show by what means the trace identified the dealer; and the address of its business premises or, for a dealer whose premises is the dealer's private residence, its county. A dealer not identified as most-traced is not described in paragraph (1), or in section 6-6, by reason of the ranking. The duties to compute and publish the ranking, to preserve the records from which it is to be computed, and to transmit and keep the copy this subsection requires, are ministerial, are owed to each person entitled to receive the published information, and are enforceable by mandamus by any person, and a published ranking that omits a dealer the records place among the most-traced dealers, or an element this subsection requires, does not satisfy the duty to publish it. The duties to preserve the records from which a ranking is to be computed, and to transmit and keep the copy, are also owed to, and enforceable by mandamus by, each person, whether or not it is or was a licensee, that those records place among the most-traced dealers, that is identified by a ranking computed from them, the published ranking, a publication under subsection (a-2), or a notice under section 6-6, or that subsection (a-1)(6) treats as described, and each party to an action concerning the ranking, including an action to compel its publication or a publication concerning it (each, in this section, a person interested in the ranking), whether or not their enforcement by any person is held invalid. A published ranking does not fail this subsection by omitting what a defended judgment or order of a court of the United States then in effect forbids it to state, if it states that an identification is omitted under a judgment or order; names each judgment or order under which anything is omitted; states, for each, the number of dealers the records place among the most-traced dealers that are omitted under that judgment or order and the number of dealers the ranking identifies for which an element this subsection requires is omitted under that judgment or order; and states the number of dealers the records so place that are omitted under any judgment or order, each counted once. Where a defended judgment or order of a court of the United States then in effect forbids the ranking to state a number the preceding sentence requires, the ranking states, in place of that number, that a judgment or order forbids it. A judgment or order excuses an omission from a ranking, or from a publication under subsection (a-2), only as this subsection and subsection (a-2)(5) state, whether or not it binds the officer who owes the duty, and the omission of any other excuse is a determination of Congress and not a gap. A judgment or order is defended, for this section, only if the court entered it on its own determination of the claim on which it was entered, over the opposition of the United States, of an officer or employee of the United States, or of a person that intervened to oppose it, on each ground on which it rests; only if no part of it was entered on a consent, stipulation, settlement, or default, or on a request no party opposed; and only if the court entered it not fewer than 21 days after the Inspector General published notice of the action under subsection (a-2)(7). A person opposes a judgment or order on a ground only by contesting its entry on that ground, in a filing or on the record, and stating why, and only if the person does not withdraw that opposition before entry; and a person that concedes or stipulates the ground, or a fact that establishes it, does not oppose on that ground. An order against identification, for this section, is a judgment or order that forbids a ranking, or a publication under subsection (a-2), to identify or state a dealer, whether it names the dealer or describes a class that includes it, or an element as to a dealer, or that directs either not to; or that directs how either is computed or verified, or the records from which either is computed or against which either is verified, by the exclusion or revision of any record or otherwise, so that either does not identify or state a dealer that the trace records the National Tracing Center held at the close from which the ranking is to be computed, as they stood at that close, place among the most-traced dealers, or an element as to such a dealer; and a fact a party stipulates or concedes does not establish, for this sentence, what those records show. In any action, including an action in a court of a State, in which an order against identification is sought or has been entered, each person interested in the ranking, and each person entitled to receive the published information, may intervene of right, at any stage of the action, including after entry and on appeal, to oppose the order, to move to vacate it, and to seek review of it, whether or not any other party does; and a motion to intervene under this sentence is not untimely by reason of the stage at which it is made. On the motion of a person the preceding sentence entitles to intervene, a court of the United States shall vacate a judgment or order, to the extent it is an order against identification that is not defended, unless the court, on its own determination over that person's opposition, finds that law other than this section requires it. Where section 3-4 of the Community Violence Reduction Act is in effect, the ranking this subsection requires to be published not later than January 31 of a year shall be treated as the identification of most-traced dealers that section 3-4(a) of that Act requires for the report due in that year, except to the extent that ranking or its publication is, on the date that report is due, held invalid or enjoined.\n\n**(a-1) Due, overdue, conducted; successors.** For this section and section 6-6:\n1. An inspection is the examination of inventory and records that section 923(g)(1)(B)(ii)(I) of title 18, United States Code, permits, and it is conducted on the day that examination is completed at the licensee's business premises. An examination under section 923(g)(1)(B)(ii)(II) of that title, a contact, a visit, or the scheduling of an examination is not an inspection.\n2. An inspection under paragraph (1) of subsection (a) is due on the later of the date twelve months after the dealer's last inspection and the date the dealer became described in that paragraph, or, for a dealer never inspected, on the date the dealer became described in that paragraph. A dealer becomes described by the ranking on the date the ranking is required to be published, whether or not it is published; and by a cited violation on the date of the report citing it. An inspection that becomes due under this paragraph remains due until it is conducted, whether or not the dealer remains described in paragraph (1) of subsection (a) on the date it falls due or after.\n3. An inspection under paragraph (2) of subsection (a) is due at the end of the period set in the SCHEDULE after the dealer's last inspection or, for a dealer never inspected, after the dealer's first license was issued.\n4. A dealer's last inspection includes an inspection conducted before this section takes effect.\n5. An inspection is overdue from the day after it is due until the day it is conducted or, if earlier, the day the dealer ceases to hold a license; the number that ceased to be overdue for that reason during the fiscal year is an element of the publication under subsection (b).\n6. A license issued to an applicant that is, or that includes as an individual possessing, directly or indirectly, the power to direct or cause the direction of its management and policies, a person who within the preceding three years was, or held that power in, a dealer that at any time within those three years was described in paragraph (1) of subsection (a) is treated, separately from that dealer, as a licensed dealer described in that paragraph, and that dealer's last inspection is its last inspection. The Attorney General may require an applicant to state the information this paragraph requires, notwithstanding the limit section 923(a) of title 18, United States Code, places on the content of an application.\n\n**(a-2) Verification by the Inspector General.**\n1. *Verification.* Not later than 60 days after the date on which a ranking subsection (a) requires is required to be published, the Inspector General of the Department of Justice shall verify the ranking against the trace records preserved under subsection (a) from which it is to be computed, and shall publish \u2014\n   (A) each dealer those records place among the most-traced dealers that the published ranking omits, stating for each the elements subsection (a) requires the ranking to state, other than the number counted only through information the dealer submitted, and with the number for which the records do not show by what means the trace identified the dealer stated as the records against which the Inspector General verified the ranking show it;\n   (B) each dealer the published ranking identifies as most-traced that those records do not so place;\n   (C) the number of records examined, stating how many are records preserved under subsection (a) and how many are records determined under paragraph (2), the number of dealers those records place among the most-traced dealers, the number of firearms counted for the last dealer so placed, the number of dealers published under subparagraph (B), and, for each judgment or order by reason of which paragraph (5) treats a dealer as not omitted, the number of dealers so treated or, where that judgment or order, while in effect, forbids the ranking to state that number, a statement that it does; and\n   (D) the records against which the Inspector General verified the ranking, and any examination the Inspector General made of when trace records were entered or revised, and how the Inspector General determined whether the preserved records are complete.\n   Where the Inspector General determines those records in whole, determines that they include each trace record from which the ranking is to be computed, and state each such identification, that the records of the National Tracing Center held at the close from which the ranking is to be computed, and publishes no dealer under subparagraph (A) or (B), the publication states that the ranking omits none and identifies none other. Where the Inspector General determines those records in whole but cannot determine that they are so complete, the publication states that it cannot, and to what extent, and does not state that the ranking omits none.\n2. *Records not preserved.* Where those records were not preserved or transmitted as subsection (a) requires, the records against which the Inspector General verifies the ranking are the trace records the National Tracing Center held as they stood at the close from which the ranking is to be computed, to the extent the Inspector General can determine them, and never those records as later revised. Where the Inspector General cannot determine those records in whole, the Inspector General publishes under paragraph (1) each dealer found in the part that can be determined, stating that each entry, and each number under subparagraph (C) of that paragraph computed from that part, is a count of that part only and, for each dealer published under subparagraph (A) of that paragraph, whether the number of firearms that part counts for it is not less than the number the published ranking counts for the last dealer it identifies or, where no ranking has been published, that none has been; states that the ranking could not be verified in whole and to what extent, and does not state that the ranking omits none.\n3. *Access and authority.* The Inspector General shall make, or at the Inspector General's election witness the making of, the copy subsection (a) requires, as the records stood at the close from which the ranking is to be computed, and the Attorney General shall permit it, including the Inspector General's review of how the records the copy contains are selected, and shall state to the Inspector General in writing, not later than 30 days before that close or, if later, the day this section takes effect, when and by what method the records will be preserved and copied; where the Inspector General made or witnessed the making of a copy, the records preserved under subsection (a) are, for this subsection, the records as that copy shows them, to the extent it shows them as they stood at that close, and the publication under paragraph (1) states the number of records in which that copy differs from the copy the Attorney General transmitted or, where the Attorney General transmitted none, that it transmitted none. The Attorney General shall make available to the Inspector General, promptly and not later than 10 days after the Inspector General requests it, the records preserved under subsection (a) and any other record the Inspector General determines is necessary to carry out this subsection, and in carrying out this subsection the Inspector General has the authority section 406(a) of title 5, United States Code, confers on an Inspector General carrying out chapter 4 of that title. The verification and the publications this subsection requires are an audit for purposes of the last sentence of section 403(a) of title 5, United States Code.\n4. *Section 413(a).* Section 413(a) of title 5, United States Code, does not place the verification or the publications this subsection requires, or the Inspector General's making or witnessing of the copy, or access to the records, that it requires, under the authority, direction, or control of the Attorney General, and does not authorize the Attorney General to prohibit, condition, or delay them. If the bar in the preceding sentence on placing them under the authority, direction, or control of the Attorney General is held invalid, the bar on prohibiting, conditioning, or delaying them stands; and if that bar is also held invalid, the duties to verify, to make or witness the making of the copy, and to publish stand. Where the President, the Attorney General, any other officer or employee who is not an officer or employee of the Office of the Inspector General, or any person acting on behalf of any of the foregoing under a contract with the United States, under any authority, including any authority claimed under the Constitution, prohibits, conditions, delays, or directs the verification, a publication, or the Inspector General's making or witnessing of the copy, or access to the records, or requests or recommends that any of them be prohibited, conditioned, delayed, or directed, whether by order, by a rule of general application as applied to any of them, or otherwise, the Inspector General shall publish, not later than 30 days after it occurs or, if later, after the Inspector General learns of it, that fact; its date; the office of the officer or employee who took the action or, for a person acting under a contract, the office for which it acted; an identification of the instrument by which it was taken and of the authority claimed for it; and any justification stated for it, omitting, and stating that it omits, information that identifies a natural person or a firearm or that concerns an ongoing civil or criminal investigation or proceeding. If the duty in the preceding sentence is held invalid as applied to an act of the President, it stands as to every other officer, employee, or person.\n5. *Omissions.* Where no ranking has been published, the ranking omits every dealer those records place among the most-traced dealers. An identification that the published ranking states is omitted under a judgment or order it names is not omitted for purposes of this subsection to the extent that judgment or order is of a court of the United States, is defended, and, while in effect, forbids the ranking to state it.\n6. *Enforcement.* The duties stated in this subsection are ministerial, are owed to each person entitled to receive the published information, and are enforceable by mandamus by any person, and a publication that omits a dealer this subsection requires it to publish, or an element or statement this subsection requires, or that states that the ranking omits none where paragraph (1) or (2) forbids it, does not satisfy it. The duty to verify, the duty to make or witness the making of the copy under paragraph (3), the duty to publish under paragraphs (1) and (2), and the duties paragraph (3) places on the Attorney General, are also owed to, and enforceable by mandamus by, each person interested in the ranking, whether or not their enforcement by any person is held invalid.\n7. *Notice of actions.* A court of the United States in which an order against identification is sought shall certify that fact to the Inspector General, with the docket number of the action and the date on which the order was first sought, and any party to the action may serve on the Inspector General a notice stating the court, that docket number, and that date. Not later than 3 days after receiving a certification or notice, the Inspector General shall publish the court, the docket number, and the date it states.\n\n**(a-3) Relief.** Relief on a claim to enforce a duty that subsection (a) or (a-2) makes enforceable by mandamus is an order directing the duty's performance; on a claim to enforce a duty that subsection (a-2) makes enforceable by mandamus, also an order declaring without effect, and directing the withdrawal of, any prohibition, condition, delay, or direction of the Attorney General to the extent paragraph (4) of that subsection bars it; a declaratory judgment; any further order necessary to carry out those orders, other than an order that restrains or delays a publication; and costs. The court shall not enter, on such a claim, an order against identification unless the order is defended. Where the court finds that such a duty has not been performed as subsection (a) or (a-2) requires, it shall order the duty's performance not later than the earliest date on which it finds the duty can be performed, and neither the defendant's other priorities, nor the resources available to the defendant, nor any other equitable consideration is a ground for withholding that order. An order directing a publication directs publication, and not a disclosure to the plaintiff alone; each such duty is owed to the plaintiff whole, and its performance in full is the complete relief to which the plaintiff is entitled. For purposes of section 2412 of title 28, United States Code, no provision of this section shall be treated as otherwise specifically providing for, or as expressly prohibiting, an award of costs, fees, or other expenses under that section; and the relief this subsection states supports no inference as to the relief on a claim to enforce a duty that any other provision of this Act makes enforceable.\n\n**(b) Publication.** The Attorney General shall publish, not later than March 31 of each year, for the preceding fiscal year, nationally and for each field division:\n1. the number of licensed dealers;\n2. the number of dealers described in paragraph (1) of subsection (a) on the first day of the fiscal year, by the ground on which each is described;\n3. the number of licenses issued during the fiscal year that subsection (a-1)(6) treats as licenses of dealers described in paragraph (1) of subsection (a);\n4. the number inspected under paragraph (1) and under paragraph (2) of subsection (a);\n5. for each of paragraphs (1) and (2) of subsection (a) separately, **the time from the date an inspection became due under subsection (a) to the date it was conducted, at the median and at the ninetieth percentile, and the number overdue at the close of the fiscal year**, and, for the inspections then overdue, the time elapsed since each became due, at the median and at the ninetieth percentile;\n6. the number of violations found, by category, and the number of inspections whose report cited a violation;\n7. each entry section 6-2(b) required to be made in its register during the fiscal year, other than the listing of licensed dealers, whether or not it was made, with the date on which it was made or a statement that it was not; and\n8. the number of inspections under subsection (a) at which section 6-6 required a comparison, the number at which the comparison was made for every quarter for which section 6-6 required it, the number of acquisitions that the records section 923(g)(1)(A) of title 18, United States Code, requires the dealer to keep show for the quarters compared, the number of inspections at which the comparison found an acquisition the dealer was required to submit under section 6-6 and did not, and the number at which it found an acquisition the dealer submitted under section 6-6 that those records do not show.\n\nThe duty stated in this subsection is ministerial, is owed to each person entitled to receive the published information, and is enforceable by mandamus by any person, and a publication that omits any element enumerated in this subsection or in subsection (a-1), or any statement subsection (c-1) requires, does not satisfy it. The first publication is made on the first March 31 after this section takes effect, and subsection (c-1)(1) applies to it in full; where this section took effect after the start of the fiscal year a publication covers, the publication covers the part of that fiscal year after it took effect.\n\n**(b-1) Relation to other law.** The publications sections 6-2(b), 6-5(a), 6-5(a-2), 6-5(b), and 6-5(c-1) require, the transmission to the Inspector General of the Department of Justice that section 6-5(a) requires, the making or witnessing of a copy and the making available of records that section 6-5(a-2)(3) requires, a notice under section 6-6, and a statement to a licensee of information it submitted under section 6-6, or of the result of the comparison that section requires, are authorized notwithstanding the sixth proviso under the heading \"Bureau of Alcohol, Tobacco, Firearms and Explosives \u2014 Salaries and Expenses\" in title II of division B of Public Law 112-55 (125 Stat. 609\u2013610; 18 U.S.C. 923 note) or any other provision of law of like effect, and amounts appropriated by section 6-5(c) may be used to prepare, publish, transmit, copy, make available, and send them. The omission from this section of the limits those provisions state is a determination of Congress and not a gap. Section 552a(b) of title 5, United States Code, does not bar any disclosure those publications require. Where a publication this section requires carries the statement section 514 of division B of Public Law 113-6 (127 Stat. 271; 18 U.S.C. 923 note) requires, that statement qualifies no duty, identification, or consequence this Act attaches to the ranking or to any other content of the publication.\n\n**(c) Appropriation.** There is appropriated, for each fiscal year, the annual amount set in the SCHEDULE for the inspections this section requires, the ranking and the publications this section requires, and the compensation of the inspectors and other personnel who perform them, permanent, available until expended, and obligated in the fiscal year for which it is made. The amount is in addition to, and shall not be derived from, any other amount made available for those purposes. Amounts appropriated by this subsection are available to the Inspector General of the Department of Justice for the verification and the publications subsection (a-2) requires, in the amount the Inspector General determines necessary; the Inspector General shall allot and obligate them, they are apportioned to the Inspector General by operation of this subsection and are not subject to any reserve, deferral, footnote, or other apportionment action or restriction, however denominated, that reduces, delays, or conditions them, an obligation or expenditure of them by the Inspector General does not violate section 1517(a) of title 31, United States Code, by reason of an apportionment, or of an amount permitted by regulations prescribed under section 1514(a) of that title, that makes available less than this subsection makes available to the Inspector General, and neither the President nor any officer or employee other than the Inspector General may withhold, defer, or condition them, including under section 1012 or 1013 of the Impoundment Control Act of 1974 (2 U.S.C. 683, 684). If their apportionment by operation of this subsection is held invalid, the President shall apportion them to the Inspector General, in the amount this subsection makes available to the Inspector General, not later than 30 days after the Inspector General requests it; and if the bar in the preceding sentence on withholding, deferring, or conditioning them is held invalid, their availability to the Inspector General stands.\n\n**(c-1) A shortfall is named, and priced.**\n1. *Staffing statement, every year.* The publication under subsection (b) shall state \u2014\n   (A) the number of full-time-equivalent inspectors who, at the average number of inspections completed per full-time-equivalent inspector over the three preceding fiscal years, would conduct the inspections that become due under subsection (a) in the fiscal year in which the publication is made, together with the inspections overdue at its start;\n   (B) the same number, computed at the average for the three fiscal years before this section takes effect;\n   (C) for each fiscal year used in subparagraph (A) or (B), the number of inspections completed, the number of full-time-equivalent inspectors, and the average examination hours per inspection; and the average cost per full-time-equivalent inspector in the preceding fiscal year;\n   (D) the number of full-time-equivalent inspectors on board at the close of the preceding fiscal year, and its difference from the number stated under subparagraph (A) and from the number stated under subparagraph (B); and\n   (E) for each difference, the increase in the amount set in the SCHEDULE for subsection (c) that would fund it at that average cost.\n2. *Shortfall label.* Where, nationally for either paragraph of subsection (a), or in any field division for both paragraphs together \u2014 (A) the inspections overdue at the close of the fiscal year the publication covers exceed the share set in the SCHEDULE of the inspections that became due during that fiscal year or were overdue at its start; or (B) the inspections conducted during the fiscal year the publication covers more than the number of days set in the SCHEDULE after they became due exceed that share of the inspections conducted during it \u2014 the publication shall state that the inspections subsection (a) requires were not conducted by their due dates, and **that is evidence that the Attorney General's inspection staffing is inadequate and shall be stated as such in the publication.**\n3. *Transmittal.* Notwithstanding section 1108(e) of title 31, United States Code, the Attorney General shall transmit the statements this subsection requires, including the personnel needs they state, with the publication under subsection (b), to the Committees on the Judiciary and on Appropriations of the Senate and of the House of Representatives. No officer or employee of an agency may condition or impair the transmittal, and nothing in this paragraph prevents the Attorney General or the President from transmitting their own views. If the bar in the preceding sentence on conditioning or impairing the transmittal is held invalid, the duty to transmit stands.\n4. *Enforcement.* The duties stated in this subsection are ministerial, are owed to each person entitled to receive the publication under subsection (b), and are enforceable by mandamus by any person. A publication that omits a statement this subsection requires does not satisfy subsection (b).\n\n**(d) No defense; no right; the ceiling stands.** A failure to inspect within a period subsection (a) states, or to make the comparison section 6-6 requires, is not a defense to any violation and creates no right in a licensee, and section 923(g)(1)(B)(ii)(I) of title 18, United States Code, continues to limit compliance inspections to one in any 12-month period."},{"anchor":"sec-6-6","heading":"\u00a7 6-6. Record information from most-traced dealers.","part":"TITLE VI \u2014 INDUSTRY ACCOUNTABILITY, DEALER SECURITY, AND EXCISE","body":"Section 923(g)(5)(A) of title 18, United States Code, applies, by operation of this section and without a letter, to each licensed dealer described in section 6-5(a)(1) of this Act, which shall submit to the Attorney General, not later than 30 days after the close of each calendar quarter, on the form used for submissions under that subparagraph or, if none is specified, in any legible form, the record information section 923(g)(1)(A) of that title requires it to keep concerning each acquisition of a firearm from a person other than a licensee made in that quarter while the dealer is so described; the duty as to acquisitions made while the dealer is so described continues after it ceases to be so described. The Attorney General may not relieve a dealer so described of this duty. The Attorney General shall send written notice to each dealer described by reason of a ranking, not later than the day the ranking is required to be published, stating the ranking, the duty this section imposes, and the day on which the record information it requires is due; the duty to send the notice is ministerial. The Attorney General shall send the same notice to each licensee treated under section 6-5(a-1)(6) as so described, with its license or, where the Attorney General learns of that treatment later, not later than 30 days after learning of it. A dealer described by reason of the ranking, or by reason of section 6-5(a-1)(6) as to a dealer so described, does not violate this section, or section 923(g)(5)(A) of title 18, United States Code, as this section applies it, by failing to submit before the earlier of the day the ranking identifying it or that dealer is published and the day the Attorney General sends it the notice this section requires, or other written notice that it is, or on a stated day will be, so described; and the record information for each quarter in which it has been so described, to the extent not already submitted, is due under that subparagraph, as this section applies it, within 30 days after that day or, if later, on the day this section otherwise makes it due. At each inspection of a dealer under section 6-5(a), the Attorney General shall compare the record information the dealer submitted under this section, for each quarter for which this section required a submission and that was not compared at an earlier inspection, with the records section 923(g)(1)(A) of title 18, United States Code, requires the dealer to keep, and the report of the inspection shall state the quarters compared, the number of acquisitions each shows for those quarters, and each acquisition that the one shows and the other does not, identified by the firearm and the date of acquisition. This section is a requirement of statute and not of rule, and neither section 926(a) of title 18, United States Code, nor the second sentence of section 923(g)(1)(A) of that title, nor the first proviso under the heading \"Bureau of Alcohol, Tobacco, Firearms and Explosives \u2014 Salaries and Expenses\" in title II of division B of Public Law 112-55 (125 Stat. 609; 18 U.S.C. 923 note) or any other provision of law of like effect, limits it, the receipt, keeping, or comparison of the information it requires, any use of that information it permits, or the use of any amounts for any of them. The preceding sentence applies to a provision of law enacted after the date of enactment of this Act unless that provision of law refers to this section and states that this section does not apply. Information submitted under this section may be used only to trace firearms, to identify patterns of trafficking, to enforce this section and section 923(g)(5)(A) of title 18, United States Code, as this section applies it, and to compute, verify, or publish a ranking or identification that section 6-5(a) or 6-5(a-2) of this Act or section 3-4(a) of the Community Violence Reduction Act requires, or a number section 6-5(b) requires, and neither that information nor a statement the report of an inspection makes under this section may be aggregated into, or used to compile, any record of firearm ownership."},{"anchor":"sec-9-1","heading":"\u00a7 9-1. Rules of construction.","part":"TITLE IX \u2014 GENERAL PROVISIONS","body":"**(a)** Nothing in this Act requires the surrender, sale, registration, or marking of any firearm or magazine lawfully possessed on the date the relevant provision takes effect, and nothing in this Act authorizes any registry of firearms or firearm owners.\n\n**(b)** No provision of this Act is to be read as part of an integrated scheme with any other provision of this Act or of any other Act; the validity of each provision is independent of every other, and no provision's invalidity informs another's.\n\n**(c)** Nothing in this Act dictates the content of Tribal law; a Tribal government joins each authority, grant, and protection on election and is bound only as to functions voluntarily assumed.\n\n**(d) A floor, and not a ceiling.** No provision of this Act, and no amendment made by this Act, is to be construed as indicating an intent on the part of Congress to occupy the field in which it operates to the exclusion of the law of any State, political subdivision, or Indian Tribe on the same subject matter, unless there is a direct and positive conflict between the provision and that law so that the two cannot be reconciled or consistently stand together. A State, a political subdivision, or an Indian Tribe may impose any requirement that is more protective than, or in addition to, a requirement of this Act, and every requirement of this Act is a minimum and not a maximum. Section 927 of title 18, United States Code, states this rule for chapter 44 of that title, and this subsection states the same rule, in the same words, for every provision of this Act that operates outside that chapter \u2014 including each section that section 9-5(d) of this Act places outside it. Nothing in this subsection enlarges or narrows the effect of section 7902 of title 15, United States Code, beyond the amendment section 6-3 makes. The omission of any broader preemptive effect is a determination of Congress and not a gap.","partHeads":[{"heading":"TITLE IX \u2014 GENERAL PROVISIONS","body":""}]},{"anchor":"sec-9-2","heading":"\u00a7 9-2. Severability and anti-aggregation.","part":"TITLE IX \u2014 GENERAL PROVISIONS","body":"If any provision of this Act, or any application of a provision, is held invalid, every other provision and application stands. No court shall treat the provisions of this Act, or of this Act together with any other Act, as an integrated scheme whose parts stand or fall together. In particular, the invalidity of any provision of Title V does not affect any provision of Titles I through IV or VI; the invalidity of section 2-3 does not affect any other section of Title II or of this Act; and the invalidity of the ex parte authority of section 4-1(c) does not affect the remainder of section 4-1."},{"anchor":"sec-9-3","heading":"\u00a7 9-3. Effective dates.","part":"TITLE IX \u2014 GENERAL PROVISIONS","body":"Sections 1-1, 2-1, and 3-4 take effect eighteen months after enactment; section 1-4(e) takes effect with section 1-1, and its first publication covers the fiscal year in which section 1-1 takes effect, from that date, whether or not any permit has been issued under section 1-4(c); sections 2-3 and 3-9 take effect 180 days after enactment; sections 5-1 and 5-2 take effect one year after enactment; section 3-8 takes effect five years after enactment, as it states; section 6-1 takes effect two years after enactment; sections 6-5 and 6-6 take effect one year after enactment; the amendments made by sections 2-2, 2-4, 2-5, 3-1, 3-2, 3-6, 3-10, 4-2, 6-3, and 6-4 take effect on enactment, subject to the window section 3-2(b) states; sections 4-1, 4-2(d), and 5-3 take effect on enactment, and a petition under section 4-2(d) may rest on a conviction entered before enactment."},{"anchor":"sec-9-4","heading":"\u00a7 9-4. Resources and continuity of operations.","part":"TITLE IX \u2014 GENERAL PROVISIONS","body":"**(a) Appropriations.** The appropriations made by sections 1-4(b), 2-5(a), 4-1(h), and 6-5(c) are permanent, are not subject to apportionment delay or programmatic withholding, and shall be obligated in the fiscal year made.\n\n**(b) Continued functions.** The federal fallback permit issuance under section 1-4(c), the permit-verification system under that section, the NICS functions of this Act, the ranking, the preservation and transmission of trace records, and the inspections section 6-5(a) requires, the verification and the publications section 6-5(a-2) requires, the duties section 6-5(a-2)(3) places on the Attorney General, and the publication duties of sections 1-4(e), 6-2(b), and 6-5(b) continue during any lapse in appropriations.\n\n**(c) Personnel.**\n1. *Who is designated.* The Attorney General shall designate the officers and employees who perform each function subsection (b) continues \u2014 the examiners who issue permits under section 1-4(c), the personnel who operate the permit-verification system under that section, the personnel who operate the NICS functions of this Act, the inspectors who conduct the inspections section 6-5(a) requires, the personnel who compute and publish the ranking, and preserve and transmit the trace records, section 6-5(a) requires, the personnel who perform the duties section 6-5(a-2)(3) places on the Attorney General, and the personnel who prepare the publications sections 1-4(e), 6-2(b), and 6-5(b) require \u2014 together with the officers and employees whose work those functions depend on, including identity verification, information technology and its security, facilities, and the hosting and transmission of the publications. The Attorney General shall designate them in the number required to perform each function at the level and within the periods this Act states for it, as if no lapse had occurred. The Attorney General shall make the designation not later than 90 days after this section takes effect, and it stands until revised.\n2. *Status by operation of law.* A designation under paragraph (1) takes effect by operation of this section at the start of any lapse in appropriations, and each designee shall be notified not later than the first day of the lapse. To the extent no designation has been made, or a designation falls short of the number paragraph (1) requires, each officer and employee who, during the pay period that includes the last day before the lapse or the pay period that includes the day sixty days before it or, for a function performed in neither, during the last pay period in which it was performed before the lapse, performed a function subsection (b) continues, or work paragraph (1) names on which such a function depends, is designated for that function by operation of this section. No review, approval, or notification by any other officer is a condition of the designation or of its effect, and the designation does not depend on any definition of the Office of Personnel Management, on any guidance or designation of the Office of Management and Budget, or on any plan. If the bar in the preceding sentence on another officer's review, approval, or notification is held invalid, the designation and its effect stand.\n3. *Pay follows the source of funds.* An officer or employee designated to perform a function financed by an appropriation this section names, or by any other source of funds that does not lapse, is paid from that source on the ordinary schedule, and section 1341(c)(2) of title 31, United States Code, does not delay that pay; to the extent the officer or employee is not so paid, that section applies. For any other function subsection (b) continues, this section specifically states, for purposes of section 1301(d) of title 31, United States Code, that obligations for the pay of the officers and employees designated to perform it may be incurred during a lapse in advance of appropriations; those obligations are authorized by law for purposes of section 1341(a)(1)(B) of that title, and the personal services of those officers and employees are authorized by law for purposes of section 1342 of title 31, United States Code; the obligations are apportioned by operation of this section and are not subject to any reserve, deferral, footnote, or other apportionment action or restriction, however denominated, that reduces, delays, or conditions them, and an obligation so incurred does not violate section 1517(a) of that title by reason of an apportionment, or of an amount permitted by regulations prescribed under section 1514(a) of that title, that makes available less than the obligation. An officer or employee designated to perform a function not financed by a source that does not lapse shall be treated as an excepted employee for purposes of section 1341(c) of title 31, United States Code, whether or not any definition of the Office of Personnel Management reaches the officer or employee.\n4. *Assignment.* During a lapse, the Attorney General may not reassign a designee from the function for which the designee was designated or change a designee's status, and shall maintain the number designated for the whole of the lapse. Nothing in this subsection limits a designee's use of leave under section 1341(c)(3) of title 31, United States Code.\n5. *Record.* Not later than the first day of any lapse in appropriations, the Attorney General shall publish the number of officers and employees designated for each function under paragraph (1), and any plan of the Department of Justice for operations during a lapse shall state the same numbers.\n6. *Post-lapse statement.* Not later than 30 days after any lapse in appropriations ends, the Attorney General shall publish, for the period of the lapse: the number of applications for a permit under section 1-4(c) received, issued, and denied, and the number issued by operation of section 1-2(a); the hours during which the permit-verification system under section 1-4(c) did not operate; and the number of inspections section 6-5(a) requires that were conducted.\n7. *Enforcement.* The duties stated in paragraphs (1), (4), (5), and (6) are ministerial, are owed to each applicant under section 1-4(c) and to each person entitled to receive the publications sections 1-4(e), 6-2(b), 6-5(a), 6-5(a-2), and 6-5(b) require, and are enforceable by mandamus.\n8. *Inspector General.* The Inspector General of the Department of Justice shall designate the officers and employees of the Office of the Inspector General who perform the verification and the publications section 6-5(a-2) requires, including the making or witnessing of a copy under section 6-5(a-2)(3), in the manner and number paragraph (1) states for the Attorney General's designations, and paragraphs (2) through (5) and (7) apply to that designation with the Inspector General in place of the Attorney General. The Attorney General may not reassign a person so designated or change that person's status.\n\n**(d) Budget statement.** For any function not permanently funded, the President's budget shall state the amount necessary to perform it fully."},{"anchor":"sec-9-5","heading":"\u00a7 9-5. Penalties.","part":"TITLE IX \u2014 GENERAL PROVISIONS","body":"**(a) Knowing violations.** Whoever knowingly violates section 1-1, 2-1(a), 2-3(a), 3-4, 3-5, 3-8(a), or 5-2(a) shall be fined under title 18, United States Code, imprisoned not more than one year, or both.\n\n**(b) Willful violations by licensees; manufacture and commercial supply.** A licensee who willfully violates a section named in subsection (a) \u2014 \"willfully\" bearing its established meaning under chapter 44 of title 18, United States Code \u2014 and whoever knowingly violates section 5-1(a), shall be fined under title 18, United States Code, imprisoned not more than five years, or both.\n\n**(c) Child access.** Whoever commits the offense stated in section 5-3 shall be fined under title 18, United States Code, imprisoned not more than five years, or both.\n\n**(d) Relation to chapter 44; no minimums.** A violation of a section named in subsection (a), (b), or (c) is not a violation of chapter 44 of title 18, United States Code, for purposes of section 924 of that title, and the amendments this Act makes to that chapter carry the penalties that chapter provides. Section 6-2(a) states the consequence of a licensee's willful violation for the license. The civil penalty stated in section 3-9(b) is the only consequence of a violation of that section. No term stated in this section is a mandatory minimum."},{"anchor":"schedule","heading":"SCHEDULE.","part":null,"body":"| Ref | Provision | Value | *(Reserved.)* |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| S-1 | \u00a7 1-2(a)(1) under-21 categories | active/reserve military; sworn law enforcement | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-2 | \u00a7 1-2(a) issue-or-deny period | 30 days | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-3 | \u00a7 1-2(c) permit fee cap | $75 | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-4 | \u00a7 1-3 safety-course hours | 8 hours | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-5 | \u00a7 1-4(b) State-program grants annual amount | $500,000,000 | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-6 | \u00a7 2-1(c) transfer fee cap | $35, indexed as the note to this SCHEDULE states | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-7 | \u00a7 4-1(h) extreme-risk implementation grants annual amount | $300,000,000 | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-8 | \u00a7 5-1(a) magazine round threshold | 10 rounds | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-9 | \u00a7 6-1 smallest-tier inventory threshold | 25 firearms | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-10 | \u00a7 6-2(a) zero-tolerance willful-violation categories | transferring a firearm to a prohibited person; failing to run a required background check; falsifying records, such as a firearms transaction form; failing to respond to a tracing request of the Attorney General; refusing, in violation of law, to permit the Attorney General to conduct an inspection | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-11 | \u00a7 6-4(a) excise increase | 10 percentage points | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-12 | \u00a7 3-8(a) conversion-device classes | drop-in auto sears; slide-mounted selector devices; trigger-group replacement devices | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-13 | \u00a7 3-9(b) lost-or-stolen civil penalty; first failure | $1,000; $250 | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-14 | \u00a7 6-5(a) number of most-traced dealers identified in the ranking; \u00a7 6-5(a) also reads Community Violence Reduction Act S-8 | 200 dealers | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-15 | \u00a7 6-5(a)(2) inspection period, all other dealers | 3 years | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-16 | \u00a7 6-5(c) inspection annual amount | $400,000,000 | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-17 | \u00a7 2-5(a) NICS record-improvement grants annual amount | $125,000,000 | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-18 | \u00a7 6-5(c-1) share of inspections overdue, or conducted late, constituting a shortfall | 10 percent | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-19 | \u00a7 6-5(c-1) days after an inspection became due beyond which it is conducted late | 30 days | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-20 | \u00a7 1-4(e) share of permit applications decided constituting an examiner shortfall | 2 percent | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-21 | \u00a7 2-1(c) daily holding charge | one-tenth of the amount S-6 states for the fiscal year in which the firearm is delivered to the licensed dealer | *(Reserved.)* |\n\n**Note \u2014 indexed amount.** The amount the SCHEDULE states for S-6 is the amount for the fiscal year in which this Act is enacted. For each later fiscal year, the amount is $35 multiplied by the product, over each later fiscal year through that fiscal year, of one plus that fiscal year's increase, the result rounded to the nearest $1. A fiscal year's increase is the Middle-Class Growth Rate defined in section 1-1 of the People's Prosperity Act, as most recently certified under section 1-3(a) of that Act before the fiscal year begins, or, where no value has been so certified within the 15 months before the fiscal year begins, including while that Act is not in effect, the percentage change in the Chained Consumer Price Index for All Urban Consumers published by the Bureau of Labor Statistics from the month 12 months before the most recent month for which it is published before the fiscal year begins to that most recent month, using each month's value as first published; an increase less than zero counts as zero. The amount that governs a transfer is the amount for the fiscal year in which the firearm is delivered to the licensed dealer. The amount changes by operation of this note, without action by any person, and the Attorney General shall publish the amount for a fiscal year before the fiscal year begins; the duty to publish is ministerial, and a failure to publish does not affect the amount."}],"asOf":"2026-11-14"}}}};
